          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following correctly pairs a fetal membrane with its primary functio</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following correctly pairs a fetal membrane with its primary function?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Amnion — produces the earliest blood cells in the embryo</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The amnion cushions the fetus and helps regulate temperature. The earliest blood cells are produced by the yolk sac, not the amnion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Yolk sac — cushions the fetus and allows free movement for limb development</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cushioning and allowing fetal movement are functions of the amniotic fluid within the amnion. The yolk sac produces the earliest blood cells and gives rise to primordial germ cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Allantois — contributes to early blood vessel formation and its remnant becomes the urachus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The allantois extends into the umbilical cord, contributes to early blood vessel formation, and its remnant persists as the urachus connecting the bladder to the umbilicus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chorion laeve — forms the fetal component of the placenta</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The chorion laeve is the smooth region where villi have regressed. It is the chorion frondosum — where villi proliferate opposite the decidua basalis — that becomes the fetal part of the placenta.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the five fetal membranes: the amnion cushions, the yolk sac makes early blood cells and germ cells, the allantois forms vessels and becomes the urachus, and the chorion frondosum (not laeve) builds the fetal placenta."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Each fetal membrane has a distinct developmental role — mixing them up is a common exam pitfall."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
