          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What feature distinguishes tertiary chorionic villi from secondary villi?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What feature distinguishes tertiary chorionic villi from secondary villi?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tertiary villi contain extraembryonic mesoderm in their core</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Mesoderm entering the villus core is the hallmark of secondary villi, not tertiary. Tertiary villi advance beyond this by developing blood vessels within that mesodermal core.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tertiary villi are composed solely of trophoblast columns</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Trophoblast-only columns describe primary villi, which appear around day thirteen. Both secondary and tertiary villi contain mesoderm in addition to trophoblast.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tertiary villi develop blood vessels within the mesodermal core</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The formation of blood vessels within the mesodermal core makes tertiary villi truly vascularised and functional for feto-maternal exchange — the critical step in establishing the placental circulation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tertiary villi are anchored to the decidua capsularis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Placental villi face the decidua basalis, not the capsularis. The decidua capsularis covers the outer surface of the gestational sac and atrophies as the uterus expands.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the three villus stages: primary villi are trophoblast-only columns (day 13), secondary villi gain a mesodermal core (week 3), and tertiary villi develop blood vessels in that core (week 3 onward), enabling actual feto-maternal exchange."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The progression from primary (trophoblast only) to secondary (mesoderm added) to tertiary (vessels formed) is a key sequence in placental development."
                }
              ],
